WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.698
[MUSIC]

00:00:06.698 --> 00:00:08.186
&gt;&gt; My name is Deb Gruenfeld and

00:00:08.186 --> 00:00:12.014
I'm a faculty member at Stanford in
the Graduate School of Business.

00:00:12.014 --> 00:00:16.106
One of my favorite classes is
called managing groups and teams.

00:00:16.106 --> 00:00:20.026
And I'm going to share five key
takeaways from that class today.

00:00:20.026 --> 00:00:24.140
&gt;&gt; [MUSIC]

00:00:24.140 --> 00:00:27.847
There's no one way to design teams for
success.

00:00:27.847 --> 00:00:31.322
You have to start with the end in mind.

00:00:31.322 --> 00:00:35.485
Often, when we talk about building a team,
we think of people first,

00:00:35.485 --> 00:00:39.457
who do I like to work with and
how can I get as many stars as possible?

00:00:39.457 --> 00:00:42.867
But we believe you can't
know who to work with or

00:00:42.867 --> 00:00:48.682
how to structure your team until you know
what's needed for the team to succeed.

00:00:48.682 --> 00:00:54.452
Teams should be designed around the clear,
engaging direction, a goal.

00:00:54.452 --> 00:00:59.392
So the building of the team is dictated,
by the specific outcomes we hope to reach.

00:00:59.392 --> 00:01:03.929
&gt;&gt; [MUSIC]

00:01:03.929 --> 00:01:08.286
&gt;&gt; When making choices about how to
design teams for different purposes,

00:01:08.286 --> 00:01:11.051
there are two key
dimensions to choose from.

00:01:11.051 --> 00:01:14.943
The first is that you have to
decide what to do about diversity.

00:01:14.943 --> 00:01:20.277
That is, what to maximize diversity
to prioritize learning outcomes and

00:01:20.277 --> 00:01:23.899
sharing different perspectives and
inclusion.

00:01:23.899 --> 00:01:25.433
Or do you want to control for

00:01:25.433 --> 00:01:29.700
the effects of diversity by making
sure team members goals are aligned.

00:01:29.700 --> 00:01:33.354
And an agreement can be reached
to the team to move forward?

00:01:33.354 --> 00:01:35.065
The second big question,

00:01:35.065 --> 00:01:39.743
in addition to what to do about
diversity is what to do about hierarchy.

00:01:39.743 --> 00:01:43.599
Whether to make sure participation and
influence are even.

00:01:43.599 --> 00:01:48.124
Or to make sure that the voices of
experts or key stakeholders are more

00:01:48.124 --> 00:01:52.268
influential than the voices of
people who are more competent.

00:01:52.268 --> 00:01:54.883
Those opinions shouldn't matter as much.

00:01:54.883 --> 00:02:00.063
Your choices will depend again, on what's
necessary for the team to succeed.

00:02:00.063 --> 00:02:04.058
&gt;&gt; [MUSIC]

00:02:04.058 --> 00:02:08.380
&gt;&gt; It's not enough to compose teams
with people who create the appearance of

00:02:08.380 --> 00:02:12.787
surface level diversity and assume that
our team will take care of itself.

00:02:12.787 --> 00:02:18.733
Unless you take steps to ensure that
the deeper level diverse perspectives and

00:02:18.733 --> 00:02:24.049
experiences and knowledge that
you're seeking actually surface and

00:02:24.049 --> 00:02:25.681
receive attention.

00:02:25.681 --> 00:02:28.479
There's a good chance that they won't.

00:02:28.479 --> 00:02:32.251
So it's important to use
processes in general,

00:02:32.251 --> 00:02:36.575
that normalize,
raising alternative points of view,

00:02:36.575 --> 00:02:40.992
asking questions and
challenging others assumptions.

00:02:40.992 --> 00:02:44.832
These things don't tend to
happen on their own in teams,

00:02:44.832 --> 00:02:50.514
especially in diverse groups where levels
of psychological safety might be loved.

00:02:50.514 --> 00:02:55.166
&gt;&gt; [MUSIC]

00:02:55.166 --> 00:02:59.229
&gt;&gt; Expertise rarely predicts
influence in teams,

00:02:59.229 --> 00:03:06.372
while things like physical attractiveness
and gender predict influence very well.

00:03:06.372 --> 00:03:09.661
In part this is because
teens fear of conflict and

00:03:09.661 --> 00:03:13.602
are motivated to reach agreement
as quickly as possible.

00:03:13.602 --> 00:03:16.628
It's important to be aware
of these dynamics, so

00:03:16.628 --> 00:03:20.310
that we can all individually
take steps to counteract them.

00:03:20.310 --> 00:03:25.323
It's good to have ways of
drawing out quieter members and

00:03:25.323 --> 00:03:29.293
gently quieting others,
to create space for

00:03:29.293 --> 00:03:33.478
less hierarchical,
more equal participation

00:03:33.478 --> 00:03:37.600
&gt;&gt; [MUSIC]

00:03:37.600 --> 00:03:42.135
&gt;&gt; Many people think it's always
better to bring a more participants in

00:03:42.135 --> 00:03:46.758
collaborative, empowering hands-off
approach to leading teams.

00:03:46.758 --> 00:03:53.200
But in reality, managing or leading
a team is a delicate balancing act,

00:03:53.200 --> 00:03:57.426
between imposing enough
top down structure and

00:03:57.426 --> 00:04:02.620
control while inviting enough
bottom up participation.

00:04:02.620 --> 00:04:08.306
Imposing direction and structure can
feel oppressive or paternalistic.

00:04:08.306 --> 00:04:13.264
But failing to articulate and
reinforce a clear vision of success

00:04:13.264 --> 00:04:17.420
can leave team members feeling lost and
unmotivated.

00:04:17.420 --> 00:04:22.043
The takeaway here is that it's
not a leaders job to manage

00:04:22.043 --> 00:04:26.200
teammates feelings or
be as likeable as possible.

00:04:26.200 --> 00:04:30.898
The job is to manage the team's
performance and outcomes in a way that

00:04:30.898 --> 00:04:36.164
engages stretches and develops the team
and guides them forward to success.

00:04:36.164 --> 00:04:46.164
&gt;&gt; [MUSIC]

00:04:49.576 --> 00:04:52.690
&gt;&gt; I was not a serious
student in high school.

00:04:52.690 --> 00:04:57.217
I mostly was interested in boys and
going to parties with my friends.

00:04:57.217 --> 00:05:02.163
So I think it has come actually as
quite a shock to many of the people who

00:05:02.163 --> 00:05:06.535
Knew me when I was younger that
that I'm a professor, yeah.

